{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Arthur blinked, the harsh sunlight stinging his eyes. He sat up in bed, a strange stiffness in his limbs he couldn't quite place. The room was… too perfect. The furniture was arranged with an unnatural symmetry, the colors were vibrant and almost garish, and a faint, looping melody played from somewhere unseen. He reached for his glasses on the nightstand, and as his fingers brushed against the cool plastic, a notification popped up in his vision: \"Quest Initiated: Retrieve Lost Family Heirloom.\" Arthur recoiled, staring at the text as if it were a hallucination. A shimmering HUD overlay appeared, displaying health, stamina, and a minimap of what looked suspiciously like his suburban neighborhood. Panic seized him. This wasn't his life. This was… a video game. He tentatively touched a nearby book, and the text within shifted, reformatting itself into a quest log entry.\n\n***\n\nHe stumbled out of bed, his mind reeling. Everything felt…scripted. The chirping of birds was too cheerful, the swaying of the trees too rhythmic. He walked to the window and looked out at his street, noticing the overly-detailed rendering of every parked car and meticulously manicured lawn. A neighbor waved, and Arthur felt a wave of nausea. The neighbor's smile was just a little *too* wide, the gesture a little *too* perfect. He tried to speak, to ask someone, *anyone*, if they noticed the uncanny feeling of artificiality, but the words caught in his throat. He was trapped, a character in someone else's meticulously crafted world, forced to play a role in a story he hadn't written. The quest log blinked insistently: \"Objective: Locate the Hidden Key.\" Arthur sighed, the weight of his digital existence settling upon him. He had a feeling this was going to be a long game.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Arthur", "logprob": -0.16696929931640625}, {"token": "▁blinked", "logprob": -0